[{:seon.task/name "the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the wiki's sections on main (`seon.doc/load-tx`), each with the references its text cites (`:seon.doc/references`, read by `seon.ref/parse`). The wiki's health is derived problems in `seon.problems` (docs/health.md), run by `bin/check health` (`bin/test --health`): `seon.problems/stale-sections` (a section citing a var whose form changed after its text), `orphan-pages`, `leadless-sections` (a first block that is no paragraph), `problem-unlinked`, `stale-pins`; a link or `path#anchor` that reaches nothing is refused at the write of the page (docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store), so it is no class. Still missing, none of these has a class: :two-homes (two sections citing one var at code depth: the sections whose `:seon.doc/references` hold the var, filtered by the depth `seon.ref/parse` reads from their text), :lead-echoes-heading (the lead's words a subset of the heading's), :lead-too-long (over 40 words, which docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept states), :lead-cites (file:line in the lead), :heading-has-code (a code span or file:line in a heading), :isolated-page (links nowhere), :changelog (a dated heading, or two or more lines starting with a date; a date in text is not one). Correct: add these as derived classes beside `seon.problems/orphan-pages`, each a read over the section facts, each linking its fix section as `:problem-unlinked` requires, with a fixture deftest per class (the three jolt/patterns fixtures are a-var-with-two-homes-is-duplication-and-names-the-home, a-lead-that-is-missing-echoes-its-heading-runs-long-or-cites-is-flagged, a-heading-with-code-an-orphan-an-isolated-page-and-a-changelog-are-flagged, from `git show 2da2255`). The measures to start from (jolt/wiki's tree): two-homes 20, no-lead 32, lead-cites 18, lead-too-long 5, heading-has-code 14, orphan 13, isolated-page 3. The style is written once, in docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/two-code-depth-homes-are-flagged seon.problems-test/a-lead-that-runs-long-or-cites-is-flagged seon.problems-test/a-heading-with-code-an-isolated-page-and-a-changelog-are-flagged}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: nothing detects \"a check that cannot fail\" (docs/testing.md#a-proof-is-focused) or a proof \"a stub would pass\". Correct: for each task's proofs, and each definition a proof reaches directly, run the proof against three stubs of that definition: (constantly nil), identity, and its first argument unchanged. A proof still green against a stub does not test that definition: a placebo, recorded as a problem fact (class :proof, naming the proof, the definition and the stub that passed). The stub is a real definition in a forked test environment holding exactly the definitions the run wants (never with-redefs or alter-var-root), on the fork path of `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`. Cost: proofs x directly reached definitions x 3 forked runs. The idea is writ's spec self-check, which rejects a law any implementation satisfies (the archived paper option3-ECOSYSTEM.md (git history); a first cut on jolt/patterns 35e1208). It would flag jolt/patterns' idiom proofs that exercised step-fns defined in the test file itself."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo seon.proofs-test/a-real-proof-fails-against-every-stub}}
 {:seon.task/name "any-markdown-file-is-docs-it-writes-back"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-01: \"Docs are like code, we import them and we have a plan for writing them back to their files if we are happy\"; \"I want docs updates as agents are working and them treating them not as files that are loading, but docs as part of the system\"; \"section is a terrible name lets swap that out for docs\"; \"I want to retain the tools for an agent to directly read from a markdown file with a filename and write to it too\"; \"any md file anywhere (full path) ideally. It's a general tool that we use internally to index and write back our own updates. So make it easy to use from our data structures\".\n\nWhat exists: docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store (one entity per heading of the wiki dirs' pages, seon.doc/load-tx and load-dir-tx at root's boot), a section a program key in the one three-way (docs/merging.md#seons-merge-is-a-three-way-merge-of-the-program), and the writer refusing a branch's own change as :seon.merge/unwritten (task seon.merge/what-merges-is-what-a-file-states, whose steps (a) to (d) this task carries out for any file).\n\nThe shape, one value for every use: a page is data, {:path p :docs [doc ...]}, each doc {:address :heading :level :text :docs [child ...]} in the page's order, the text the only thing written and everything else (lead, references, names, read) derived by the one parse as load-tx derives it. The same value is what my.doc/page (not built) answers, what my.doc/write! (not built) takes, what a cited page is under @ctx (so a swap! of it is the edit) and what the render draws; seon.doc/page-text of it is the file, byte for byte.\n\nSteps, in order: (1) rename :seon.doc/* to :seon.doc/* (36 files, 261 uses on 2026-11-01; the word section stays in text for a page's part), (a boot loads no page now: a renamed store is indexed again by a seed or a re-index). (2) the round-trip: store each doc's level and position, and the text before a page's first heading, so seon.doc/page-text of a loaded page is its file; proof seon.doc-test/load-then-page-text-is-a-zero-diff over every page of docs/ and AGENTS.md, as seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff is for src. (3) any path: my.doc/page and my.doc/write! (to build; seon.doc/page is the db read today) of a full path (a path in the checkout stored relative to it, so worktrees and generations agree; any other absolute), loading that one page into the agent's branch and touching no other page (md-scan 29's one-page load swept 556 sections), write! taking the page value or its text and refusing as edit! refuses, with the parse's problem named; a plain spit into the checkout from an agent's REPL is refused naming write!. (4) write-back: a merge's changed pages are written as its changed namespaces are (seon.source/changed-files, seon.fx/git-commit on the merges branch); a file outside the repository is written by seon.fx/write-file when its text on disk is still the text it was loaded from, else it is an unmerged entry (base, ours, and the file as theirs); :seon.merge/unwritten goes. (5) readers read the branch's db, never the file. Then the fence bench task, parked on this.\n\nFound at root's REPL, 2026-11-01: (i) the store already holds a page byte for byte but for order: a page drawn from its sections, each heading at its depth (# per level), a blank line after it, its text, a blank line, is its file exactly for 28 of the 38 pages; the ten others differ by a doubled or missing blank line (render.md, source.md, health.md's trailing blank among them) or by the order the draw guessed. Children by entity id put docs/agent.md#working-a-task last, though it is the page's second heading, so the one new attribute is :seon.doc/position; level is the depth and needs no attribute, and the ten pages are made regular once. (ii) the renders need no new path: every part of #p-task but ctx-keys already answers data, a cite's model render (seon.render.ui/cite-model) sends a doc as {:seon.print/plain ...} Markdown and its html render (cite) draws the same text as HTML; a call vector [#'f arg] prints as the call, and a text argument prints as its Markdown, while an eager (markdown text) prints its :div and :class hiccup. (iii) the natural edit is the one agents know: edit! of a doc's address with [[old new] ...] as of a var (seon.repl/edit!), the page then loaded by load-tx so the derived attributes follow; a swap! of :seon.doc/text through @ctx (seon.ctx/write!) would write the text and leave lead, references and names as they were, so it reaches load-tx the same way. The doc cite says how, beside its :read, as a schema cite says :change.\n\nBuilt on jolt/docs-any-md, 2026-11-01: the rename (seon.doc aliased doc: a base program analyzed beside the image keeps its own docs alias, bin/check health), :seon.doc/heading as written, /level and /position, seon.doc/markdown with seon.doc-test/a-loaded-page-draws-back-as-its-file over the whole wiki (seven pages made regular), and seon.repl/edit! of a doc's address (seon.doc/doc-source, edit-tx; docs/agent.md#changing-a-doc; seon.repl-test/a-doc-is-edited-as-a-definition-is). Left: step (3)'s any path and my.doc/write!, step (4) write-back, step (5).\n\nThe owner, 2026-11-01, next: \"we may need to refactor how we are indexing and the format so we have a better representation that isn't just the files parsed, but is the most useful way for us to piece docs together into context that will help the agent. I want the ability to curate and come up with targeted docs for whatever we need to teach the agents and to have the task templates be mostly great info off the shelf that an agent can customize.\" The sessions' proposal, from what exists (docs/references.md#an-entity-cites): (6) a doc's citations are :seon.cite entities of the doc, as a task's plan's are (seon.task/cites-tx), so one citation representation serves tasks, agents and docs, and :seon.doc/references, /names and /dead-links become reads of them; (7) a curated doc is a doc whose text is mostly citations at their depths: cited whole, its own cites come into the page one level down, as a task's needs' do, each once at the strongest depth any asks (seon.render.ui/page-cites); (8) each kind's instructions in seon.template/instructions become a doc (a templates page), a task made citing its kind's template as an agent is made citing :seon.config.agent/cites (seon.task/agent-cites-tx), so the agent customizes it with my.agent/cite! and uncite!, edits its plan, and edit!s the template doc itself when the change is general; (9) the vaguer-task step: a task whose plan cites little, its first step finding and citing its references, which the person sees in the side list. Checked 2026-11-06: the three proofs named are green. Step (4) for pages in the repository is built since (f39b5c3521: a merge writes back its changed doc pages, the :seon.merge/unwritten refusal removed; seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back). Not built: my.doc/page and my.doc/write! (src/my/doc.clj holds only reviewed!), a file outside the repository, steps (5) to (9)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/a-loaded-page-draws-back-as-its-file seon.repl-test/a-doc-is-edited-as-a-definition-is seon.merge-test/a-branch-doc-edit-merges-and-its-page-is-written-back}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-text-index-is-a-secondary-index"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the FTS5 text index (`seon.sqlite`, `seon.doc/text-index`) is one process-wide slot outside Datahike, keyed by a db's :hash and :max-tx, moved by hand by `seon.doc/carry-index!` from the three callers that know what a move wrote (seon.agent's transact! and merge of main, the fork server's commit of main); a db value other than the slot's is answered by a whole rebuild. Datahike already has the mechanism for this, the secondary-index protocol (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/index/secondary.cljc: ISecondaryIndex, ITransientSecondaryIndex, the registry `register-index-type!`, `-transact` called per datom inside the transaction for the attributes a schema entry with :db.secondary/type names). Its contract is that an index value is persistent: an older db value searches its own text. Mutating one SQLite in place, as the scriptum adapter does (src-secondary/datahike/index/secondary/scriptum.clj), would answer a `d/with` speculation or an older value with newer datoms, and Seon writes speculatively everywhere (seon.ctx's writes, the judge, the fork server's start). The design, the shape of Lucene's segments: an index value is a sealed base (an FTS5 database built from a db's text datoms, shared by every value derived from it and by forked children, cached and evictable, rebuilt from the db at t when gone: derived data: docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on) and a persistent delta {[e a] text-or-nil} its transact fills (no native call per datom; a speculative value costs a map entry). A search on a value applies its delta to the base inside a savepoint (entries of [e a] deleted, the delta's inserted), queries, and rolls back, so bm25 is the corpus' own exactly; one search at a time per base (the savepoint is the base's). The holder that keeps values long (the fork server for main) rebases when its delta passes a configured entry count, and a value read from the store holds no base and builds one on its first search. No IVersionedSecondaryIndex: nothing is persisted, as now. The query reaches it through `seon.doc/tokens`, `seon.doc/grep` and `scores` with the db in the query spec, and through a Datalog clause with :datahike/external-engine metadata (modelled on proximum's `nearest`) so an agent joins text matches with datoms. It deletes `seon.doc/carry-index!` and its callers, the `seon.doc/indexes` slot, and the use counting of `seon.sqlite` (retire!, using) where a base's lifetime becomes the cache's. The fork (a jolt/<topic> branch of seantempesta/datahike): entity_set.cljc's `:clj` branch is RoaringBitmap with an (int eid) cast, and Seon's entity ids are 64-bit content ids (up to ~1.15e18 on the standing root), so a `:jolt` branch of a set of longs comes first, and secondary.cljc's instance? check of RoaringBitmap reads it. Measured 2026-11-03, read-only from the eval records (:fns): in the two cc7e L2 sessions an agent read the index 21 times in 69 evals and 12 in 80, and every read but at most one reused its carried slot (at most 133k ticks and 211 ms per read; the rerun's second eval, 1.4 s, is the one possible build). So carry-index! keeps an agent fresh, and step 2 saves an agent little time. The standing root does not carry main's writes: all 8 of its evals that searched rebuilt the whole index (about 3M ticks in FTS5, 450 to 2370 ms per eval), and its render proc draws an agent page's search on that agent's branch db, so by its code (not measured) root pays one rebuild for each change it draws. Probed at the REPL (2026-11-02): d/with reaches -transact once per datom (a retraction, then an assertion), and each value searches its own entries. sqlite3_serialize copies a 65 MB FTS index in 4.6 ms, and sqlite3_deserialize (FREEONCLOSE|RESIZEABLE) opens the copy in 0.25 ms, independent of the original, where a build took 794 ms. So a search can seal its own copy (base plus delta) with no savepoint, at the cost of native memory freed only at GC. A db read from the store (an agent's d/connect, the fork server's commit-as-db, a merge base) gets an empty skeleton, so the cache keyed by [:hash :max-tx] stays for it. Index status :building backfills asynchronously when a schema entry is added to attributes that already hold datoms, so the index is declared in a fresh store's seed schema (the standing store is erased once). Risks to settle first, at the REPL: every path that makes Seon's db values goes through Datahike's transaction (d/with, d/db-with, d/transact: yes; a store read gives a value with an empty skeleton: the lazy base covers it); `-transact` must tolerate a retraction of an entry it never held; the savepoint must survive a fiber switch between its statements (Jolt's engines preempt by ticks)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.doc-test/a-speculative-value-searches-its-own-text seon.doc-test/an-older-value-searches-its-own-text seon.doc-test/a-value-read-from-the-store-builds-its-base-on-its-first-search seon.doc-cost-test/a-transaction-writing-a-text-costs-its-datoms-not-the-index}}
 {:seon.task/name "what-a-first-search-allocates-after-a-long-history"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-05 by the control's merge gates. seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited went red only after enough earlier tests on a shared root: running all of seon.agent-test, a first grep allocated 14.6-15.2 MB against a second's 3.4 MB and a build's 20.7 MB (on the gates, 20.4-21.2 MB and 3.5 MB); neither half of the 36 tests before it was enough alone. It is not a rebuild: the agent's seon.doc/text-index slot was at the agent's db. So the test took a root of its own (until 2026-11-10: it asserts no bytes now, and runs on the shard's root), and what allocates the first grep's extra bytes is unknown. Find it with seon.profile/measure! in a run of the whole namespace; the node cache going cold is a guess. Correct: said what it is, and fixed if a first search costs an agent more than it should."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agents-first-search-reads-the-index-it-inherited}}
]
